;;; A relation parameter its activator binds to `eq` heads a premise of one
;;; argument, and the run stopped where the plan is compiled — **W60**,
;;; regression `eq-arity-premise` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule `r` whose parameter `?R` heads the premise `(?R ?x)`, and its
;;; activator `(r eq)`, which binds `?R` to `eq`. `eq` is the relation of
;;; equality, between exactly two values (Q-M1e2.26), so the
;;; premise is `(eq ?x)`, which the grammar refuses wherever it is written.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(eq ?x)` written in the `:match` is a parse error. Written through the
;;; parameter, the activator the program states binds it, so the loader sees
;;; it: the program is refused at load (Q-M1f.46), one line naming `r`, `?R`,
;;; the form and the activator, and every command that loads it exits 1
;;; (`ein test`: 2). A run that stops is no answer.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loaded, and every command stopped at root where `(r eq)`'s
;;; plan is compiled, exit 1, with the compiler's line in the Python IR's
;;; spelling, *`(eq …)` takes exactly 2 arguments and was given 1:
;;; SForm(head=Var(name='R'), …) at Loc(…)*, the compiler's arity check
;;; (`ein solve FILE` at `ee3da657`, release and debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The loader refuses the program: every command that loads it exits 1 with
;;; one line, naming `r`, `?R`, the form and `(r eq)` (`ein test`: 2), in
;;; either build. The regression's `now` is that refusal; the section above is
;;; the record of the answer it replaced.
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit")
(p a)
(rule r (?R)
  :match (?R ?x)
  :assert (hit ?x)
  :why "{?x} stands in {?R}, so it is hit")
(r eq)
(query :goal (hit ?x))
